\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs28\b Special Features 1.7: places that may need special formatting\b0\fs22\par
Special Features is a Paratext 9 plugin (Tools > Special Features...) that lists the places in the Bible where published translations often use special layout, and shows whether your project already formats them. It only reads the project; it never changes the text.\par
\par
\b WHY\b0\par
Genealogies, lists, letters, prayers, inscriptions, acrostic psalms and similar passages read better when they are set apart from ordinary paragraphs, for example Matthew 1 as an indented list or "THE KING OF THE JEWS" in Mark 15:26 as an inscription. These places are easy to miss while translating. The tool collects them in one list so that a team, a consultant or a typesetter can check them before publication.\par
\par
\b WHERE THE LIST COMES FROM\b0\par
The places come from the paper "Special Layout Features found in Old and New Testaments" (2002, revised 2004), which compared seven Bibles: NIV, NVI, NLT, GNB, TEV, BFC and CEV. Entries of the same kind whose verses overlap are joined, giving about 400 places in 17 kinds: genealogies, lists, tables, letters and decrees, prayers, special prose, days of creation, Ten Commandments, inscriptions, centered text, acrostics (Hebrew letters), speakers named, refrains, numbered sayings, major and minor divisions, and textual notes. A second list adds about 220 places where the New Testament quotes the Old Testament, with their own "OT quotations" tick box.\par
\par
